{
  "findings": [
    {
      "question": "claims_supported",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The core arithmetic claims (C1, C2, C5, C7, C8, C15) are accurately sourced. But three supporting claims carry more than their citations bear. (a) C4 asserts the engaged-member count basis is 'unaffected by the closure'. Nothing cited establishes that; the closure removed 214,000 accounts migrated from Pellworth only six months earlier, so the numerator series may also have a composition break at the same boundary. If it does, the recommendation's own 'un-broken metric' is broken too. (b) C10 states 'The cost of the counter write-back is unmeasured. No estimate exists in evidence' and cites the platform constraints document, which says nothing about cost — it says only that the ledger has no replacement and refreshes four-hourly. Absence of evidence is being cited as if it were evidence. (c) C11 quotes a three-point redemption series (47.9 / 41.3 / 38.6) but the middle figure sits in a record not cited (EV-d652bd7c), and C6 quotes the current base of 1,781,000 against the segmentation record rather than the metric row that holds it (EV-dce9d700). Separately, the two sources for the closure disagree on magnitude: the operations log records a day-of-run fall of 2,646,000 to 1,728,000 while the quarterly series shows 2,638,000 to 1,742,000. That discrepancy is unremarked, and it is an input to the very restatement being recommended.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a",
          "quote": "918,000 accounts were closed, of which 214,000 were accounts migrated from Pellworth Chemists in April."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a",
          "quote": "The active-member base fell from 2,646,000 to 1,728,000 on the day of the run."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-6b4ee1ba",
          "quote": "value: 1742000"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1345e218",
          "quote": "No replacement is scheduled."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-d652bd7c",
          "quote": "value: 41.3"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-dce9d700",
          "quote": "value: 1781000"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "contradictions_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Most recorded conflicts are engaged head-on and correctly: the rate-versus-count divergence, the earn-rate blocking position, the untested app claim, the prescription-data prohibition, the stale segmentation. Two conflicts are dropped. First, the operations log's day-of-run base figures do not reconcile with the quarterly closing-base series, and the restatement depends on which is authoritative. Second, and more consequentially, the recommendation leans on the engaged-member count as the clean series while the same closure run removed 214,000 recently migrated accounts — the possibility that the numerator series is also discontinuous is never tested, even though it would weaken both the 'trend is flat-to-declining' hypothesis (H1) and the claim that the count basis needs no restatement. Third, the governance conflict is only half engaged: the policy bars use of any data originating in the dispensary system for marketing segmentation, targeting or personalisation, and the recommendation asserts a scan-event-only feed 'can be scoped to avoid this' without any evidence that legal-privacy accepts that reading.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-630bd154",
          "quote": "Dispensing records, prescription history and any data originating in the dispensary system may not be used for marketing segmentation, targeting or personalisation."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a",
          "quote": "918,000 accounts were closed, of which 214,000 were accounts migrated from Pellworth Chemists in April."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-791b9ede",
          "quote": "value: 2638000"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "preference_as_evidence",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "Stakeholder positions are handled unusually well: the Retail Operations reading, the Programme Director's earn-rate case and the Digital Lead's usability claim are all labelled as opinion and none is used as a finding. Two design assertions are nonetheless carried as though established. First, that a 'scan-event-only' extract from the dispensary environment is scopeable within the current consent — that is the author's design intent, not a legal-privacy position, and the six-week DPIA lead time is the downside if it is wrong. Second, ALT-05 being 'designed to be margin-neutral' is repeated in the statement as a property of the option; the evidence supports only that route change does not alter stated earn or redeem value, and any lift in the redemption rate has a realised cost the finance position may capture. The conditions ask finance to confirm, which is correct, but the statement should not present margin neutrality as a feature.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-630bd154",
          "quote": "Assessments currently take six weeks from submission."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-ed36a688",
          "quote": "\"No net increase in promotional margin this year. That includes anything funded out of the points pool.\""
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "non_ai_considered",
      "verdict": "passes",
      "explanation": "The non-AI field is genuinely the substance of this analysis, not a formality. ALT-05, ALT-06 and ALT-07 each receive dimension-by-dimension assessment including honest 'cannot_assess' entries where Brackenhill has no effect-size evidence, and the selected option is itself a non-AI reporting and integration change. The AI options (ALT-11, ALT-12) are the ones argued down, on consent and instrumentation grounds rather than dismissively.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1345e218",
          "quote": "The rebuilt app has no feature-flag or A/B framework."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "no_build_considered",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "ALT-01 (no change), ALT-02 (defer to FY27) and ALT-03 (Wave 4 research) are all present and each carries a substantive, sceptical assessment — that part is real. What is missing is any engagement with them in the recommendation statement itself. The statement explains why ALT-09, ALT-10 and ALT-12 lose; it never says why ALT-04 beats deferral or research. That gap matters because the deferral case is strong on the same evidence the recommendation relies on: no experimentation framework, weekly-only member tables, 96 of 436 stores unable to transact loyalty until 2026-Q4, and a segmentation frozen at June 2024 with Wave 4 never commissioned. ALT-03 also addresses more of the listed 'would_change_recommendation' gaps than ALT-04 does. A reader cannot see why the measurement-only option won.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-1345e218",
          "quote": "96 of the 436 stores are still on the Pellworth EPOS build and will not read the Care Club barcode."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-043ac12d",
          "quote": "built on the Care Club member base as at 30 June 2024 and not refreshed since"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-31a8e867",
          "quote": "Wave 4 was scheduled for autumn 2024 and was not commissioned."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "overconfident",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The support narrative is candid about what is not evidenced, which is to its credit, but 'moderate' still overstates the case for the recommendation as written, for three reasons. (1) Half the selected option — the dispensing-counter write-back — has no cost estimate, no owner agreement, and an unresolved governance reading; a recommendation should not carry moderate support for a component whose feasibility is entirely unassessed. (2) The decision-relevant conclusion that the recommendation exists to deliver ('the 40.4% is a denominator artefact') is already demonstrated by the evidence in hand: the rate and the count move in opposite directions across the boundary and the closure is documented. Re-briefing the review meeting on that does not require a prioritised workstream, so the incremental value of ALT-04 over simply presenting the existing count series is unproven. (3) The sequencing claim — 'do this ahead of any member-facing change' — has no evidential support and carries a real cost: FY26 ends 31 August 2026 and the review meeting was held 21 July 2026, so 'do measurement first, pilot ALT-05 within the FY26 window' is arithmetically close to impossible. Lower to low, or narrow the statement to the restatement-and-dual-reporting element only and drop the 'ahead of any member-facing change' sequencing.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb",
          "quote": "FY26 runs from 1 September 2025 to 31 August 2026."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-ed36a688",
          "quote": "Meeting held: 2026-07-21"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-462f83a6",
          "quote": "value: 709000"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-4096796d",
          "quote": "value: 704000"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a",
          "quote": "Reporting was not restated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_could_make_it_wrong",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "The six listed falsifiers are mostly good, but they miss the two most likely ways this is a mistake. First, the calendar: FY26 runs to 31 August 2026 and the review was held 21 July 2026, so there is no FY26 window left in which a restatement can be followed by a member-facing pilot. If the real decision on the table is the FY27 plan, then sequencing measurement 'ahead of any member-facing change' costs nothing and the recommendation is harmless but also nearly content-free; if the decision is what to do in the next six weeks, the recommendation spends the whole of it on reporting. Either way the stated rationale is wrong. Second, redundancy: if the review meeting can be corrected today using the existing engaged-member count series and the documented closure, the restatement project delivers no decision change and the priority should have been the member-facing candidate. Third, and specific: if some of the 214,000 closed Pellworth accounts had earn or redeem events in 2025-Q2 or 2025-Q3, the engaged-member count series also breaks at the closure boundary, the 'count basis is unaffected' premise fails, and H1's framing ('the underlying trend is flat-to-declining') would need re-deriving before it could retire the 'do more of what worked' reading. Fourth: if the dispensary till system cannot emit scan events without the very integration being scoped, H2 is untestable at low cost and the second half of the option cannot be gated as the conditions assume.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb",
          "quote": "FY26 runs from 1 September 2025 to 31 August 2026."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-fd0252d0",
          "quote": "not measured; the dispensing counter till does not write loyalty scans back to the member account"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a",
          "quote": "The volume is not known."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "question": "what_to_test",
      "verdict": "concern",
      "explanation": "H1 is not a test, it is a recomputation of data already held by analytics; it can and should be done before any prioritisation decision rather than as the deliverable of one, and it should include a restated numerator (engaged members excluding the closed cohorts) not only a restated denominator. H2 is the load-bearing proposition for the expensive half of the option and it is stated without a method: there is no evidence that dispensing-counter scan volume can be sampled without building the feed whose justification depends on the answer. Before any money is committed the tests should be: (a) can the dispensary till system produce a one-off, read-only scan-count extract for a sample of stores and a single month, and at what effort — get an engineering estimate in writing; (b) a written legal-privacy ruling that a scan-event-only feed used for engagement counting is not 'use for marketing segmentation, targeting or personalisation', obtained before scoping work, since the fallback is a six-week DPIA; (c) pharmacy operations' agreement that this does not cross the FY26 scope boundary; (d) a dry run of the corrected briefing using existing data, to establish whether the restatement project changes any decision that the existing count series does not already change; (e) a finance ruling on whether reinstated till coupons at unchanged offer values are margin-neutral, since that determines whether ALT-05 runs in parallel rather than after.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-630bd154",
          "quote": "A separate, explicit, purpose-specific consent is required and must be recorded per member."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-630bd154",
          "quote": "Assessments currently take six weeks from submission."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb",
          "quote": "Dispensing services, prescription delivery and the clinical services timetable are owned by the pharmacy operations team and are not part of the loyalty programme."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "reclassify": [
    {
      "claim_id": "C4",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "The quarterly counts are facts, but the claim's decision-relevant content — that the count basis 'is unaffected by the closure' — is an inference. The October 2025 run closed 214,000 accounts migrated only in April 2025, so a composition break in the numerator is possible and untested. Labelled 'fact', this inference lets the recommendation treat the count series as a clean baseline it has not verified."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C10",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "'The cost of the counter write-back is unmeasured' is a statement about the state of the evidence base, not a technical constraint of the platform, and the cited record (EV-1345e218) contains no cost content. Labelling an evidence gap as a constraint makes it look surveyed when it has simply not been asked."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "C12",
      "new_type": "assumption",
      "reason": "That occasional_convenience is 'the largest segment by headcount' and coupon-motivated is only a fact about the 30 June 2024 base of 2,262,000 members; the current base is 1,781,000 after a 918,000-account closure that would largely have removed the dormant segment. Whether it is still the largest segment today is assumed, and the claim's own caveat says so — the type should match."
    }
  ],
  "recommended_support": "low",
  "what_would_change_it": [
    "If the restated numerator (engaged members excluding both closed cohorts) shows the count series is itself discontinuous at October 2025, the 'clean metric' premise behind C4 fails and H1 must be re-derived before the review meeting can be re-briefed.",
    "If the FY26 calendar is confirmed as ending 31 August 2026, the stated rationale — avoiding 'spending FY26 on the wrong intervention' and pilot-after-restatement inside FY26 — is not available, and the decision is really about FY27 sequencing.",
    "If the existing engaged-member count series is sufficient to retire the 'do more of what worked' reading at the next meeting, the restatement project delivers no decision change and the member-facing candidate should have been prioritised instead.",
    "If the dispensary till system cannot emit a sample scan count without the full integration, the second half of ALT-04 cannot be gated as the conditions assume and should be dropped from the recommendation rather than staged.",
    "If legal-privacy reads engagement counting from dispensary-originated scan events as falling inside the prohibition on segmentation and targeting use, the write-back requires a DPIA and is out of reach on any near-term timeline."
  ],
  "what_to_test": [
    "Recompute the engagement series with both numerator and denominator restated across the April 2025 Pellworth load and the October 2025 closure, and reconcile the operations log's day-of-run figures (2,646,000 to 1,728,000) with the quarterly series (2,638,000 to 1,742,000) before publishing anything.",
    "Obtain a written engineering estimate for a one-off, read-only dispensing-counter scan-count extract for a sample of stores and one month, before committing to any integration.",
    "Get a written legal-privacy ruling that scan-event-only counting of dispensary-originated events is outside the marketing segmentation, targeting and personalisation prohibition — before scoping work, given the six-week DPIA fallback.",
    "Get pharmacy operations' written confirmation that the feed does not cross the FY26 out-of-scope boundary.",
    "Dry-run the corrected briefing to the review meeting using data already held, to establish empirically whether the restatement workstream changes any decision the existing count series does not.",
    "Get a finance ruling on whether reinstated till coupons at unchanged offer values are promotional-margin neutral, including the realised cost of any rise in redemption rate, so ALT-05 can be assessed in parallel rather than assumed to follow."
  ]
}

loyalty_programme_refresh::baseline::v2.text:
{
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "C-01",
      "statement": "The number of members who actually earn or redeem has fallen in every measured quarter, from 748,000 in 2024-Q2 to 691,000 in 2026-Q2.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-4d552aab", "quote": "value: 748000", "locator": "engaged_members_quarter, 2024-Q2"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-6e72fa57", "quote": "value: 726000", "locator": "engaged_members_quarter, 2025-Q1"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-fe848c99", "quote": "value: 691000", "locator": "engaged_members_quarter, 2026-Q2"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Nine consecutive quarterly rows from the analytics export, all pointing one way. This is the counted outcome the decision question asks about, and it is unaffected by base-size changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-02",
      "statement": "The apparent jump in engagement rate to 40.4% in 2025-Q4 coincides with the active-member base falling from 2,638,000 to 1,742,000 after the dormant-account closure run, and reporting was not restated.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-8733325c", "quote": "value: 40.4", "locator": "engagement_rate, 2025-Q4"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-791b9ede", "quote": "value: 2638000", "locator": "active_members_base, 2025-Q3"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-6b4ee1ba", "quote": "value: 1742000", "locator": "active_members_base, 2025-Q4"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a", "quote": "Reporting was not restated. The engagement rate series in the analytics export uses the closing active-member base for each period.", "locator": "2025-10-15 dormant account closure"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "The denominator change is documented in the operations log and visible in the series. The rate rose while the numerator continued to fall."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-03",
      "statement": "Head of Retail Operations believes engagement is already recovering because the rate went from 26.9% to over 40% and held.",
      "claim_type": "stakeholder_opinion",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ed36a688", "quote": "Engagement is already recovering. The rate went from 26.9% to over 40% and it has held for three quarters. Whatever we did in the autumn worked, and we should do more of it.", "locator": "Head of Retail Operations"}
      ],
      "opposing_citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-4096796d", "quote": "value: 704000", "locator": "engaged_members_quarter, 2025-Q4"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a", "quote": "The active-member base fell from 2,646,000 to 1,728,000 on the day of the run. 918,000 accounts were closed, of which 214,000 were accounts migrated from Pellworth Chemists in April.", "locator": "2025-10-15"}
      ],
      "support_level": "low",
      "rationale": "A confidently recalled result that the underlying measurement disagrees with: the numerator kept falling and the denominator was cut by 918,000 accounts. It remains an opinion, held by a senior stakeholder."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-04",
      "statement": "Member share of transactions has fallen from 41.2% (2024-Q2) to 37.1% (2026-Q2), against an FY26 goal of 45%.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-604a0bc0", "quote": "value: 41.2", "locator": "member_share_of_transactions, 2024-Q2"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-1065de60", "quote": "value: 37.1", "locator": "member_share_of_transactions, 2026-Q2"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb", "quote": "Grow member share of transactions to 45%.", "locator": "Annual goals"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Till-scan share is measured directly and is not affected by the account-closure run. The gap to goal is widening."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-05",
      "statement": "Points redemption rate fell from 47.9% in 2025-Q4 to 41.3% in 2026-Q1 and 38.6% in 2026-Q2, the two quarters after till coupon printing was switched off.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-d332e2bf", "quote": "value: 47.9", "locator": "points_redemption_rate, 2025-Q4"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-d652bd7c", "quote": "value: 41.3", "locator": "points_redemption_rate, 2026-Q1"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-53b966b8", "quote": "value: 38.6", "locator": "points_redemption_rate, 2026-Q2"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a", "quote": "Coupon printing at the till was switched off in all stores on the same day the own-brand price change took effect. Redemption is now available in the app or by colleague override at the counter.", "locator": "2026-01-05"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "The numbers and the dates are both documented. The timing coincidence is a fact; the attribution is not (see C-06)."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-06",
      "statement": "Attributing the redemption decline to the removal of till coupons is an inference, not a measurement: the earn-rate cut and the coupon switch-off happened on the same day, and the redemption-rate denominator (points earned) was changed by the earn-rate cut itself.",
      "claim_type": "assumption",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-fd279c64", "quote": "The own-brand earn rate was reduced from 4 points per pound to 1 point per pound to fund the price reduction, and till coupon printing was retired at the same time.", "locator": "D-2026-01"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-39e162c1", "quote": "share of points earned in the period that were redeemed - synthetic", "locator": "points_redemption_rate definition"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Two simultaneous changes with no control group means neither effect was isolated. The ratio's denominator also moved, so the direction of the mechanical effect is not obvious."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-07",
      "statement": "In the 2023 member survey, 79% of respondents' redemptions were made with a till-printed coupon and 14% in the app; the survey was 1,204 members with a 22% response rate and has not been repeated.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-31a8e867", "quote": "79% of redemptions in the survey period were made with a coupon printed at the till. 14% were made in the app. 7% were applied manually by a colleague at the counter.", "locator": "Redemption behaviour"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-31a8e867", "quote": "Online survey of 1,204 Care Club members, quota-sampled against the member base as at September 2023 by age band, region and store format. Response rate 22%.", "locator": "Method"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-31a8e867", "quote": "Wave 4 was scheduled for autumn 2024 and was not commissioned.", "locator": "Wave 4"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "A fact about a quota-sampled survey of members in late 2023, based on self-report. It was a fact when written; the channel it describes no longer exists, so it cannot be read as a current population figure."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-08",
      "statement": "The largest member segment on the 2024 segmentation — occasional_convenience, 587,000 members, 26.0% of the base, 21.0% engagement — had 'coupon printed at the till' recorded as its top stated motivation.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-c8c04c10", "quote": "members: 587000", "locator": "occasional_convenience, as_of 2024-06-30"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-c8c04c10", "quote": "top_stated_motivation: coupon printed at the till", "locator": "occasional_convenience"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-c8c04c10", "quote": "engagement_rate_percent: 21.0", "locator": "occasional_convenience"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-043ac12d", "quote": "note: synthetic - built on the Care Club member base as at 30 June 2024 and not refreshed since", "locator": "all_segments row"}
      ],
      "support_level": "moderate",
      "rationale": "Accurate as at 30 June 2024 and explicitly not refreshed since; it predates both the account-closure run and the January 2026 changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-09",
      "statement": "Finance has ruled out any net increase in promotional margin this year, explicitly including anything funded out of the points pool, and any proposal that increases the cost of points or discounts needs finance approval before a pilot can begin.",
      "claim_type": "business_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ed36a688", "quote": "No net increase in promotional margin this year. That includes anything funded out of the points pool.", "locator": "Finance Director"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb", "quote": "Promotional margin is the binding constraint for FY26. Any proposal that increases the cost of points earned or discounts given requires finance approval before a pilot may begin, and no net increase in promotional margin is available this year.", "locator": "Priorities and constraints"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Stated both in the FY26 objectives document and restated by the Finance Director in the July 2026 review."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-10",
      "statement": "Dispensing and prescription data may not be used for marketing segmentation, targeting or personalisation under Care Club consent; a separate per-member consent is required.",
      "claim_type": "governance_constraint",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-630bd154", "quote": "Dispensing records, prescription history and any data originating in the dispensary system may not be used for marketing segmentation, targeting or personalisation. Care Club membership consent does not cover this use. A separate, explicit, purpose-specific consent is required and must be recorded per member.", "locator": "Dispensing and prescription data"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "Current policy, owner legal-privacy, last updated 2026-03-10."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-11",
      "statement": "The Digital Lead's proposal to personalise off prescription data is the direction she prefers; it runs directly into the dispensing-data prohibition and the FY26 scope exclusion for dispensing services.",
      "claim_type": "stakeholder_opinion",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-ed36a688", "quote": "The app rebuild fixed the usability problems the 2023 study found. That is done. The next thing is personalisation off prescription data, because we know what people take.", "locator": "Digital Lead"}
      ],
      "opposing_citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-630bd154", "quote": "Care Club membership consent does not cover this use.", "locator": "Dispensing and prescription data"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-0770c8bb", "quote": "Dispensing services, prescription delivery and the clinical services timetable are owned by the pharmacy operations team and are not part of the loyalty programme.", "locator": "Out of scope for FY26"}
      ],
      "support_level": "low",
      "rationale": "A senior preference. Seniority does not convert it into evidence, and the governance policy contradicts the mechanism she proposes."
    },
    {
      "id": "C-12",
      "statement": "No usability study has been run against the rebuilt app, so the claim that the rebuild fixed the 2023 usability problems is unmeasured rather than confirmed.",
      "claim_type": "fact",
      "citations": [
        {"evidence_id": "EV-22f8864a", "quote": "No usability study has been run against the rebuilt app.", "locator": "2025-11-12 App rebuild release"},
        {"evidence_id": "EV-31a8e867", "quote": "Only 3 of the 42 interviewees could find their points balance in the app without prompting. Eleven abandoned the attempt.", "locator": "Friction"}
      ],
      "support_level": "strong",
      "rationale": "The operations log states plainly that the study does not exist. The original problem was measured; the fix was not."
    },
